[00:14.04]The city of Oxford has got a traffic problem.
[00:17.58]It's an old town and the streets are narrow.
[00:20.58]There are too many cars and not enough parking spaces in the city centre.
[00:25.73]The Planning Department wanted to change the traffic system, and so they conducted a traffic survey.
[00:31.67]They asked a lot of people these five questions:
[00:34.66]<br>
[00:35.66]1 How old are you?
[00:38.68]2 Can you drive?
[00:41.50]3 How long have you been able to drive?
[00:45.60]4 Where do you live?
[00:48.58]5 How do you come to town?
[00:52.54]<br>
[00:53.14]Here are some of the results:
[00:56.20]Mr Brown's fifty-eight.
[00:58.70]He learned to drive when he was eighteen.
[01:01.48]He's been able to drive for forty years.
[01:04.58]He lives in the country, twenty miles from Oxford.
[01:08.06]He always comes into town by car.
[01:11.26]<br>
[01:11.90]Mary Mackintosh is twenty.
[01:14.18]She's had a lot of driving lessons.
[01:16.74]She's taken the driving test three times, but she hasn't been able to pass the test yet.
[01:22.46]She lives near the city centre, and she usually walks to work.
[01:26.74]<br>
[01:27.74]Bob Brewer's twenty five.
[01:30.34]He's been able to drive for two years, but he hasn't got a car.
[01:34.56]He hasn't been able to save enough money.
[01:37.36]He lives in a village outside Oxford.
[01:40.08]He comes into Oxford by train.
[01:43.02]<br>
[01:43.72]Mr and Mrs Wilson are both over sixty-five.
[01:47.25]They've never been able to drive.
[01:49.34]They've never learned.
[01:51.22]They live in a suburb of Oxford and they occasionally come into town by bus.